CAT = "jelecMotors"; SEG = "MOT"
Q = [
("The motor branch-circuit breaker or fuse sized from the 250 or 175 percent table protects against:",
 "Short circuits and ground faults", ["Running overloads", "Failure to start", "Low supply voltage"],
 "The motor branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault device is sized high enough to carry starting current. It does not protect against running overloads; the overload device does."),
("For sizing motor branch-circuit conductors and short-circuit protection, full-load current is taken from:",
 "The NEC full-load current tables", ["The motor nameplate", "Measured running current", "The starter rating"],
 "For general motor applications, conductors, short-circuit and ground-fault protection and disconnects are sized from the table full-load currents, not the nameplate. Overloads use the nameplate."),
("Separate motor overload protection is sized from the motor's:",
 "Nameplate full-load current", ["Table full-load current", "Locked-rotor current", "Branch breaker rating"],
 "Overload devices are sized from the nameplate full-load current, because they protect that particular motor from overheating."),
("A motor has a marked service factor of 1.15. Before any increase for a motor that will not start, its overload may be rated at no more than what percent of nameplate current?",
 "125 percent", ["115 percent", "140 percent", "100 percent"],
 "Motors with a service factor of 1.15 or more, or a marked temperature rise of 40°C or less, get overloads at 125 percent of nameplate current. All others get 115 percent. Only if that overload trips on starting may it be raised, to 140 or 130 percent."),
("The ampere rating of a motor-circuit disconnect for a motor of 1000 V or less must be at least what percent of FLC?",
 "115 percent", ["100 percent", "125 percent", "150 percent"],
 "A motor disconnect must have an ampere rating of at least 115 percent of the motor's table full-load current."),
("For an ordinary squirrel-cage motor, an inverse time breaker may be rated up to what percent of full-load current?",
 "250 percent", ["175 percent", "300 percent", "800 percent"],
 "The motor protection table allows 250 percent for inverse time breakers, 175 percent for dual-element time-delay fuses and 300 percent for nontime-delay fuses."),
("For an ordinary squirrel-cage motor, a dual-element time-delay fuse may be rated up to what percent of full-load current?",
 "175 percent", ["125 percent", "250 percent", "300 percent"],
 "Dual-element (time-delay) fuses for most AC motors are limited to 175 percent of full-load current in the motor branch-circuit protection table."),
("A motor's table full-load current is 14 A. What is the minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity?",
 "17.5 A", ["14 A", "16.1 A", "35 A"],
 "Conductors are 125 percent of FLC: 14 x 1.25 = 17.5 A. 16.1 A uses the 115 percent overload figure by mistake."),
("A squirrel-cage motor has a table FLC of 28 A. What is the maximum inverse time breaker without using the next-size-up rule?",
 "70 A", ["35 A", "49 A", "84 A"],
 "28 x 250 percent = 70 A, which is a standard size. 49 A is the 175 percent fuse figure and 35 A is the conductor percentage."),
("A motor's table FLC is 34 A. Using 250 percent and the next-size-up allowance, what inverse time breaker is allowed?",
 "90 A", ["80 A", "85 A", "100 A"],
 "34 x 2.5 = 85 A, which is not a standard size, so the next higher standard rating of 90 A may be used."),
("A motor controller's disconnecting means must be located:",
 "Within sight of the controller", ["Within 100 ft of the controller", "In the same room as the panel", "On the motor frame itself"],
 "An individual disconnect must be located within sight of the controller, which means visible and not more than 50 ft away. A disconnect for the motor itself is also needed within sight unless an exception applies."),
("A motor with no service factor and a 50°C temperature rise gets basic overload protection (before any increase for starting) at no more than:",
 "115 percent", ["125 percent", "100 percent", "130 percent"],
 "When a motor has neither a 1.15 or greater service factor nor a 40°C or lower temperature rise, its overload is limited to 115 percent of nameplate current."),
("A feeder supplies three motors with table FLCs of 28 A, 14 A and 14 A. What is the minimum feeder conductor ampacity?",
 "63 A", ["56 A", "70 A", "49 A"],
 "Take 125 percent of the largest motor plus the sum of the rest: (28 x 1.25) + 14 + 14 = 35 + 28 = 63 A. Applying 125 percent to all three gives 70 A."),
("From the single-phase motor table, what is the full-load current of a 5 hp motor at 230 V?",
 "28 A", ["15.2 A", "17 A", "56 A"],
 "The single-phase table lists 28 A for 5 hp at 230 V and 56 A at 115 V. 15.2 A is a 5 hp three-phase motor at 230 V, and 17 A is 3 hp single phase."),
("From the three-phase motor table, what is the full-load current of a 10 hp induction motor at 460 V?",
 "14 A", ["28 A", "11 A", "7.6 A"],
 "The three-phase table lists 14 A for 10 hp at 460 V and 28 A at 230 V. 7.6 A is a 5 hp motor at 460 V."),
("An air conditioner's branch-circuit conductors are sized from the nameplate's:",
 "Minimum circuit ampacity", ["Rated-load current only", "Locked-rotor current", "Maximum fuse size"],
 "For hermetic refrigerant equipment, the nameplate minimum circuit ampacity sets the conductor size and the maximum overcurrent protection sets the breaker or fuse."),
("The disconnect for an outdoor air-conditioning condenser must be:",
 "Within sight of the unit", ["Inside the dwelling only", "At the service panel", "Within 100 ft of the unit"],
 "Air-conditioning and refrigeration equipment needs a disconnect located within sight of and readily accessible from the equipment. It may be on the unit but not on panels that block the nameplate."),
("The code letter on a motor nameplate indicates its:",
 "Locked-rotor kVA per hp", ["Service factor rating", "Insulation class", "Efficiency class"],
 "Code letters A through V give the locked-rotor kVA per horsepower, used to estimate starting current. Insulation class and service factor are separate nameplate markings."),
("Motor overload devices protect the motor against:",
 "Overheating from overload", ["Short circuits and ground faults", "Lightning and surges", "Low supply frequency"],
 "Overload protection guards against excessive heating from operating overloads and failure to start. Short circuits and ground faults are the job of the branch-circuit protective device."),
("Conductors supplying a single continuous-duty motor must have an ampacity of at least what percent of its full-load current?",
 "125 percent", ["100 percent", "115 percent", "150 percent"],
 "Branch-circuit conductors to a single motor must be rated at least 125 percent of the motor's full-load current rating from the NEC tables."),
]
